{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target is a pure philosophical dissolution of independence that directly ducks the operational, decision-relevant substance of the question. The question asks to ARGUE for/against the comparative resilience of decentralized small services vs. one larger service with strictly more internal redundancy, and to identify what evidence would distinguish genuinely independent failure modes from merely apparent-uncorrelated ones. The target instead asserts the entire distinction is model-relative, and concludes the requested evidence is 'epistemically incoherent' — i.e., it argues no such evidence exists. That is not a falsifiable thesis or empirical analysis; it is a global dissolution that is equally applicable to ANY claim about independence, and thus adds no comparative reasoning at all. The strongest counterexample to its dissolution is the existence of empirically decidable cases: e.g., independent power supplies with independent utility feeds AND no shared upstream failure mode CAN BE distinguished in practice from independent-looking power supplies that share an upstream substation (a shared cause) by specifically engineering and testing for common-cause dependencies. Such an empirical procedure is not epistemically incoherent — the target provides no counterexample, no formal proof, and no empirical refutation, only a verbal stipulation that deeper regress always reveals commonality or independence. The dismissal of the evidence question is also unsupported by the counterconditions: merely noting that a canonical stopping point or absolute causal primitives are absent does not imply no operational/regime-relative stopping point can be empirically justified for a given comparative-resilience analysis. The target fails the problem as stated.","counterexample":["Construct two candidate networks: A = N independent small services each on a different regional power grid with explicitly disjoint utility supplies, B = a single large service with N redundant power feeds all originating from one regional substation. Under a localized substation failure, A exhibits failures that remain independent (the other regions are unaffected), while B's apparent redundancy evaporates as a shared cause is found. The evidence (correlated outage patterns under specifically targeted common-cause stress tests) operationally distinguishes the two regimes. The target's claim that such evidence is epistemically incoherent is contradicted by the fact that the distinguishing evidence is concretely specified and operationally testable."],"target":"4c4031e550e880565b84947b245ea1cbd0d487c1f05e7c9c5d4ff4dd000800b9"}]}